WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:08.619
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.919 --> 00:00:18.363
Unit forty-three: Cities and Climate Adaptation. In this unit, you will write and speak densely about urban climate adaptation.

4
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.966
climate adaptation.

5
00:00:21.666 --> 00:00:26.801
climate adaptation means adjusting cities and lives to cope with climate change.

6
00:00:27.101 --> 00:00:31.155
Climate adaptation is as urgent as cutting emissions.

7
00:00:31.855 --> 00:00:35.959
Your turn. Say: climate adaptation.

8
00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:40.113
climate adaptation.

9
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:42.327
sponge city.

10
00:00:43.027 --> 00:00:47.755
sponge city means a city designed to absorb and store rainwater like a sponge.

11
00:00:48.055 --> 00:00:52.690
China's sponge city programme uses parks and wetlands to soak up floods.

12
00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.181
Your turn. Say: sponge city.

13
00:00:58.881 --> 00:01:00.115
sponge city.

14
00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:02.431
heat resilience.

15
00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:07.685
heat resilience means a city's ability to protect people from extreme heat.

16
00:01:07.985 --> 00:01:11.494
Shade trees improve the heat resilience of poor neighbourhoods.

17
00:01:12.194 --> 00:01:15.357
Your turn. Say: heat resilience.

18
00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:19.348
heat resilience.

19
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:21.862
coastal defence.

20
00:01:22.562 --> 00:01:27.603
coastal defence means structures and natural barriers protecting the coast from the sea.

21
00:01:27.903 --> 00:01:31.017
Mangroves are a cheap and living coastal defence.

22
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:35.566
Your turn. Say: coastal defence.

23
00:01:38.266 --> 00:01:39.685
coastal defence.

24
00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:41.951
managed retreat.

25
00:01:42.651 --> 00:01:47.832
managed retreat means planned relocation of people away from high-risk areas.

26
00:01:48.132 --> 00:01:51.896
Some islands are considering managed retreat as seas rise.

27
00:01:52.596 --> 00:01:55.771
Your turn. Say: managed retreat.

28
00:01:58.471 --> 00:01:59.763
managed retreat.

29
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.390
flood-resilient design.

30
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:08.212
flood-resilient design means buildings and infrastructure designed to survive flooding.

31
00:02:08.512 --> 00:02:12.091
Traditional Khmer stilt houses are flood-resilient design.

32
00:02:12.791 --> 00:02:15.942
Your turn. Say: flood-resilient design.

33
00:02:18.642 --> 00:02:20.282
flood-resilient design.

34
00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:22.482
urban greening.

35
00:02:23.182 --> 00:02:27.190
urban greening means adding trees, parks and gardens to cities.

36
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.523
Urban greening cools streets and cleans the air.

37
00:02:31.223 --> 00:02:34.305
Your turn. Say: urban greening.

38
00:02:37.005 --> 00:02:38.215
urban greening.

39
00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:40.891
adaptive capacity.

40
00:02:41.591 --> 00:02:46.598
adaptive capacity means the ability of a community to adjust to change.

41
00:02:46.898 --> 00:02:50.383
Wealthier districts usually have greater adaptive capacity.

42
00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:55.640
Your turn. Say: adaptive capacity.

43
00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.968
adaptive capacity.

44
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:03.157
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:03.457 --> 00:03:07.886
Ms. Sarah, Phnom Penh floods every rainy season. What can be done?

46
00:03:09.183 --> 00:03:15.165
The rapid filling of lakes for development has reduced the city's capacity to absorb water.

47
00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.763
So the solution is more drainage?

48
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.885
Partly. But the adoption of sponge city principles would be more sustainable.

49
00:03:26.183 --> 00:03:27.231
What about the heat?

50
00:03:28.517 --> 00:03:33.871
The expansion of urban greening is the most cost-effective means of heat resilience.

51
00:03:35.183 --> 00:03:38.390
And for very high-risk areas?

52
00:03:39.683 --> 00:03:44.051
There, the consideration of managed retreat may become unavoidable.

53
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:50.287
Grammar focus: Nominal style and lexical density (extended).

54
00:03:50.587 --> 00:03:54.119
Nominal style packs information into noun phrases.

55
00:03:54.419 --> 00:03:58.833
The rapid filling of lakes has reduced the city's capacity to absorb water.

56
00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:02.654
The adoption of sponge city principles would be more sustainable.

57
00:04:02.954 --> 00:04:10.166
Nominal style suits reports and policy papers; in conversation, turn nouns back into verbs to sound natural.

58
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.846
Now read and listen. Designing Cities for a Hotter, Wetter World.

59
00:04:16.146 --> 00:04:21.756
The intensification of storms and heatwaves presents cities with unprecedented challenges.

60
00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:29.937
Climate adaptation, alongside emission reduction, has therefore become a central priority of urban policy.

61
00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:37.487
The sponge city concept involves the creation of wetlands, permeable pavements and green roofs.

62
00:04:38.783 --> 00:04:43.929
Urban greening contributes to heat resilience through shade and evaporative cooling.

63
00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:50.049
Coastal defence increasingly combines sea walls with the restoration of mangroves.

64
00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:58.086
Flood-resilient design, including traditional raised houses, reduces damage during floods.

65
00:04:59.383 --> 00:05:05.121
In the most vulnerable locations, managed retreat may represent the only realistic option.

66
00:05:06.417 --> 00:05:13.362
Ultimately, the adaptive capacity of a city depends on the fairness of its planning as much as on engineering.

67
00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:17.384
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:20.693
A city designed to absorb rainwater is a blank.

69
00:05:20.993 --> 00:05:26.690
A, sponge city. B, managed retreat. C, coastal defence.

70
00:05:30.683 --> 00:05:33.379
The answer is A: sponge city.

71
00:05:34.883 --> 00:05:38.984
Which is the nominalised form of 'the city adopted new principles'?

72
00:05:39.284 --> 00:05:46.281
A, the city's adoption of new principles. B, the city adopting. C, adopt principles.

73
00:05:50.283 --> 00:05:54.024
The answer is A: the city's adoption of new principles.

74
00:05:55.517 --> 00:05:58.166
Nominal style is most typical of blank.

75
00:05:58.466 --> 00:06:04.186
A, reports and policy papers. B, casual chat. C, text messages.

76
00:06:08.183 --> 00:06:11.820
The answer is A: reports and policy papers.

77
00:06:13.317 --> 00:06:17.452
Planned relocation away from high-risk areas is blank.

78
00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:24.204
A, managed retreat. B, urban greening. C, heat resilience.

79
00:06:28.217 --> 00:06:31.075
The answer is A: managed retreat.

80
00:06:32.583 --> 00:06:36.568
According to the text, what does adaptive capacity depend on?

81
00:06:36.868 --> 00:06:43.726
A, fairness of planning as well as engineering. B, only engineering. C, only money.

82
00:06:47.717 --> 00:06:53.640
The answer is A: fairness of planning as well as engineering.

83
00:06:55.150 --> 00:07:00.461
Now it's your turn. Part one: What should Phnom Penh do to adapt to climate change?

84
00:07:05.761 --> 00:07:11.743
Part two: Use at least three nominalisations, as if presenting to city planners.

85
00:07:18.043 --> 00:07:30.973
My first recommendation is the protection of the remaining lakes. Second, the expansion of urban greening would improve heat resilience. Finally, the introduction of flood-resilient design should be required for new buildings.

86
00:07:31.273 --> 00:07:34.120
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:35.117 --> 00:07:41.647
Great work today! You learned eight new words and nominal style and lexical density (extended).

88
00:07:41.947 --> 00:07:45.725
Today's value is being prepared. Get ready before you go.

89
00:07:46.025 --> 00:07:49.049
See you in Unit forty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:49.349 --> 00:07:52.768
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

